course: biology
lesson: 94
title: Parrots, flamingos and the bird of prey show
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A parrot says «hello» because in the wild it copies the calls of its flock to belong, and in a zoo or a home the people become its flock.
explain: Flamingos look as if they are doing everything wrong: they stand on one leg, eat with their heads upside down and are only pink because of what they eat, yet each habit has a good reason.
explain: At the bird of prey show an owl drifts over the crowd without a sound, a falcon drops onto a swinging lure, and a hawk comes back to the glove because it has learned the glove means food.
explain: A vulture lands last, bald and scruffy, but it is one of nature's cleaners, eating dead animals before their germs can spread.
explain: Cartoons bend these birds: Rio's Blu is a real kind of macaw, but Road Runner would lose a real race against a coyote, and real owls do not deliver letters.
fact: Parrots copy sounds, including words, to fit in with their flock; an African grey parrot called Alex learned to name colours, shapes and numbers up to six.
fact: Macaws have strong hooked beaks that crack nuts too hard for people to break by hand, and parrots also use the beak like a third foot to climb.
fact: Macaws in the Amazon gather in hundreds at riverbank clay licks, because the clay gives them salt that is hard to find in the rainforest.
fact: Big parrots can live 50 years or more, need lots of company and can scream loudly, so many pet parrots end up in rescue centres.
fact: Flamingos often rest on one leg, even asleep; it takes almost no muscle effort for them, and the backwards-bending joint is really the ankle.
fact: Flamingos get their pink from pigments in the algae and tiny shrimps they eat, so zoos add the pigment to their food; baby flamingos are grey.
fact: A flamingo feeds with its head upside down in the water, filtering tiny food through comb-like edges of its bill.
fact: Owls fly almost silently thanks to soft fringed feather edges, and an owl can turn its head about three quarters of the way round, but not a full circle; most owls hunt at night, and no owl can be trained to carry letters.
fact: In a dive the peregrine falcon is the fastest animal, reaching more than 300 km per hour, and peregrines nest on tall city buildings, which are high like their wild cliffs and full of pigeons to eat.
fact: Birds of prey are trained with food rewards and come back to the glove because it pays; the thick leather glove protects the trainer from the talons.
fact: Vultures have bare heads that stay clean when they feed inside dead animals, and their strong stomach acid kills germs that would make other animals ill.
fact: Blu in the film Rio is a Spix's macaw; that bird was declared extinct in the wild in 2019, and birds bred in captivity were reported released in Brazil from 2022.
fact: A real roadrunner can run at about 30 km per hour, but a real coyote can run about twice as fast.
exercise: Why do parrots copy sounds? | To fit in with their flock | Because they understand every word | To scare away hungry snakes | To ask passing people for food
exercise: What did Alex the African grey parrot learn to do? | Name colours, shapes and numbers up to six | Read whole books aloud | Sing every song he heard | Count all the way up to a thousand and back
exercise: What is a macaw's strong hooked beak good for? | Cracking hard nuts and climbing | Digging deep holes in the ground | Catching slippery fish deep underwater | Sucking sweet nectar from flowers
exercise: Why do wild macaws eat clay from riverbanks? | The clay gives them salt | They are very hungry for mud | It cleans their beaks after eating | It keeps their feathers bright
exercise: How long can a big parrot live? | 50 years or more | About 5 years | About 2 years | About 200 years
exercise: Why should a family think hard before buying a parrot? | It lives for decades, needs company and can scream | It must eat meat every day | It always bites children, so it must live in a cage | It cannot live indoors at all
exercise: Why can a flamingo stand on one leg so easily? | It takes almost no muscle effort for it | One of its legs is much stronger than the other | It holds onto the mud with its toes | It leans against other flamingos
exercise: What is the backwards-bending joint on a flamingo's leg? | Its ankle | Its knee | Its hip | Its toe
exercise: Why do zoos add something to flamingo food? | To keep their feathers pink | To make them grow taller | To stop them flying away | To make their eggs hatch faster
exercise: What colour is a baby flamingo? | Grey | Bright pink | Deep red | Pale blue
exercise: How does a flamingo eat? | Head upside down, filtering tiny food with its bill | Catching fish with its beak like a heron | Pecking seeds one by one from the long grass | Tearing meat with its claws
exercise: Why can't you hear an owl fly? | Its feathers have soft fringed edges | It glides only downhill | It holds its breath the whole time it is flying | It flies only very slowly
exercise: Which animal is the fastest of all in a dive? | The peregrine falcon | The cheetah | The owl | The vulture
exercise: Why does a trained hawk come back to the trainer's glove? | It has learned the glove means food | It loves the trainer like a dog and misses them | It is tied to the glove | It is too tired to fly further
exercise: Why is a vulture's head bald? | It stays clean when feeding inside dead animals | Its head feathers simply fell out as it got older | It keeps the vulture cool in the snow | It frightens other birds away
exercise: How do vultures help other animals? | They clean up dead animals before diseases spread | They chase hungry lions away from the herds | They carry seeds to new places | They warn of storms
exercise: What kind of bird is Blu in the film Rio? | A Spix's macaw | A bright scarlet ibis | An African grey parrot | A keel-billed toucan
test: 6 flamingos asleep each on one leg. how many feet touching the water | 6 | 12 | 3 | 18
test: My little brother says parrots know everything they say. Best answer? | They copy sounds; some, like Alex, learned simple meanings | Yes, parrots understand every single word they say | No, parrots cannot make any sounds | Yes, parrots learn English at school
test: the flamingos have gone all pale, keeper whats wrong with em | Their food is missing the pink pigments | They are cold and frightened of all the visitors | They are very old | They have been in the sun too long
test: Your friend says an owl can spin its head right round like in a cartoon. What is true? | It turns about three quarters of the way, not a full circle | Yes, it spins round and round | No, owls cannot turn their heads at all, only their eyes | It turns only a little, like a person
test: In Harry Potter, owls carry letters to people's homes. What is true of real owls? | They hunt at night and cannot be trained as postmen | They will deliver your letters if you give them a stamp | They carry parcels heavier than themselves | They fly only in daylight
test: Road Runner always gets away from Wile E. Coyote. What would happen in a real race? | The coyote, about twice as fast, would usually win | The roadrunner would win easily every time | The roadrunner would fly far away | They could never meet, as they live on different continents
test: A peregrine nests on a cathedral roof in the city. Why might it like it there? | It is high like a cliff, and city pigeons are food | It is warm inside, and it likes listening to the organ | It hides from the rain in the bells | People feed it every day
test: What would happen if the trainer stopped giving the hawk meat? | It would probably stop coming back to the glove | It would keep coming back anyway, out of love for the trainer | It would learn to talk | It would start eating seeds
test: A sign in Brazil says Spix's macaws were declared extinct in the wild in 2019. Why are some flying there again? | Birds bred in captivity were released from 2022 | They had just been hiding deep in the rainforest all along | Scientists cloned them from feathers | They flew back from another continent
test: A parrot climbs up the cage bars. How many toes point forward on each foot? | Two | Three | Four | Only one
test: Why is a vulture useful even if people think it is ugly? | It cleans up dead animals and stops disease spreading | It eats the rats and beetles that ruin farmers' crops | It pollinates flowers | It guards the herds from lions
exam: Identify the principal reason for which flamingos maintained in captivity are provided with supplementary pigment. | Their pink colouring derives from pigments obtained in the diet | The pigment strengthens the long, thin legs upon which they stand | The pigment prevents them from flying | The pigment accelerates the hatching of eggs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the flight of the owl? | It is nearly silent, owing to the soft fringed edges of its feathers | It is conducted solely at great altitude | It is accompanied by a loud whistling produced by the stiff wing feathers | It is possible only in daylight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the training of hawks. | The bird returns because it has learned the glove signifies a food reward | The bird returns out of genuine affection for the trainer, as a dog does | The bird is incapable of flight beyond the field | The bird returns owing to fatigue
exam: It is given that four flamingos stand upon one leg apiece whilst two further flamingos stand upon both legs. Ascertain the aggregate number of feet in contact with the ground. | Eight | Six | Twelve | Ten
